FINAL: This report examines the global expansion of battery energy storage capacity during 2025 alongside the shifting economic drivers that accelerated market adoption. It reviews deployment totals, regional additions, and detailed pricing metrics before outlining the primary economic and technological factors that transformed project feasibility. Readers can navigate this analysis through dedicated sections covering deployment metrics, chemistry dominance, cost trends, and regional breakdowns.

# Executive Summary

Battery storage has established itself as the fastest growing power technology globally. According to the International Energy Agency’s Global Energy Review 2026, published on April 20, worldwide developers installed 108 GW of new battery storage capacity in 2025. This represents a 40% increase year-on-year compared to 2024, achieving an annual expansion rate that surpassed the highest yearly expansion ever recorded for gas-fired power generation capacity. Total installed capacity reached a level eleven times higher than its 2021 baseline. The global lithium-ion battery market exceeded USD 150 billion in 2025, marking an increase of over 20% from 2024.

# Key Findings

Nearly 80% of new installations in 2025 involved large-scale grid-connected projects, underscoring the rise of centralized storage solutions designed to support intermittent renewable energy sources like solar and wind. Lithium-iron phosphate (LFP) batteries dominated the technology landscape, accounting for approximately 90% of deployments due to lower costs and efficient cycling capabilities, although they remain less energy-dense than rival chemistries commonly used in electric vehicles (EVs). 

Storage duration evolved significantly across the sector. While developers continued to favor two-hour systems, storage duration extended as systems offering four hours or more became increasingly common. The average storage duration of new utility-scale projects rose from roughly two hours in 2023 to three hours in 2025 to support higher shares of solar and wind generation.

Operationally, batteries transitioned from backup equipment into active grid resources performing multiple functions simultaneously, such as frequency regulation and energy shifting:
* In California, which installed more than 17 GW of battery capacity, batteries supplied over 40% of the state’s electricity demand during the evening peak on March 29, 2026, and contributed over 60% of hourly ramping requirements.
* In the ERCOT market, batteries supplied over 40% of hourly ramping needs during April 2026.
* In regions with high renewable shares, batteries contributed more than 30% of hourly ramping requirements during several months of the year.

Geographically, China remained the world’s largest battery storage market in 2025, accounting for nearly 60% of newly installed capacity by adding more than 63 GW across large utility-scale projects and distributed storage systems. The United States followed with 19 GW of additions, while Europe installed around 6.2 GW with a strong shift toward utility-scale deployments. Australia experienced remarkable growth by installing nearly 8 GW of new battery capacity—roughly nine times more than the previous year—with battery storage reaching around 18% of the country's installed dispatchable generation capacity. Additional fast-growing markets included Saudi Arabia in the Middle East, Chile, and African nations such as South Africa, Egypt, Senegal, and Togo.

