# Economic Drivers

Project economics improved significantly as battery costs fell by more than 90% between 2010 and 2025, driven by technological advancements and scaling manufacturing. This improved the business case for utilities, industries, commercial buildings, and renewable developers without heavy reliance on subsidies. Optimal global markets achieved utility-scale Levelized Cost of Storage (LCOS) as low as $65/MWh (per Ember's October 2025 analysis), making BESS directly competitive with natural gas peaker plants.

Development timelines for utility-scale battery projects averaged approximately 275 days for construction, mirroring solar projects and offering a much faster deployment cycle than gas-fired plants or large infrastructure projects, though permitting and grid connections often extended total timelines.

In contrast, regional divergence occurred in the US market, where Foreign Entity of Concern (FEOC) rules and supply chain restructuring drove a 23% price increase in utility-scale BESS and a divergence from global pricing trends.

