## Global Battery Storage Additions and Economic Drivers in 2025

According to the International Energy Agency’s Global Energy Review 2026, published on April 20, global battery electricity storage achieved record-breaking growth in 2025. Developers installed 108 GW of new battery storage capacity worldwide during the year, representing a 40% increase year-on-year—an annual expansion rate that surpassed the highest yearly expansion ever recorded for gas-fired power generation capacity. This surge brought total installed capacity to a level eleven times higher than its 2021 baseline. The global lithium-ion battery market exceeded USD 150 billion in 2025, marking an increase of over 20% compared to 2024.

Nearly 80% of new installations in 2025 involved large-scale grid-connected projects, highlighting the rise of centralized storage solutions designed to support intermittent renewable energy sources such as solar and wind. Lithium iron phosphate (LFP) batteries dominated the technology landscape, accounting for approximately 90% of deployments due to their lower costs and efficient cycling capabilities. Storage duration also evolved: while developers continued to favor two-hour systems, configurations offering four hours or more became increasingly common, with the average storage duration of new utility-scale projects rising from roughly two hours in 2023 to three hours in 2025.

Project economics improved significantly as battery costs fell by more than 90% between 2010 and 2025. This reduction was driven by technological advancements and scaling manufacturing, which improved the business case for utilities, industries, commercial buildings, and renewable developers without heavy reliance on subsidies. Furthermore, development timelines for utility-scale battery projects averaged approximately 275 days for construction, mirroring solar projects and offering a much faster deployment cycle than gas-fired plants or large infrastructure projects, though permitting and grid connections often extended the total timeline.

Geographically, China remained the world’s largest battery storage market in 2025, accounting for nearly 60% of newly installed capacity by adding more than 63 GW across large utility-scale projects and distributed storage systems. The United States followed with 19 GW of additions, while Europe installed around 6.2 GW with a strong shift toward utility-scale deployments. Australia experienced remarkable growth by installing nearly 8 GW of new battery capacity—roughly nine times more than the previous year—with battery storage reaching around 18% of the country's installed dispatchable generation capacity. Other regions also accelerated adoption, including Saudi Arabia in the Middle East, Chile, and African nations such as South Africa, Egypt, Senegal, and Togo.

Operationally, batteries transitioned from backup equipment into active grid resources performing multiple functions simultaneously, such as frequency regulation and energy shifting. For instance, in California, which installed more than 17 GW of battery capacity, batteries supplied over 40% of the state’s electricity demand during the evening peak on March 29, 2026, and contributed over 60% of hourly ramping requirements. Similarly, batteries supplied over 40% of hourly ramping needs in the ERCOT market during April 2026, and contributed more than 30% of hourly ramping requirements during several months of the year in regions with high renewable shares.
